#{[17592186399774 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186399775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399774 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112863" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399774 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399774 #edb/ref 1071 "According to the rational roots theorem, which of the following could be a root of the polynomial $f(x)=7{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-14?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399774 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8da2d1e9-08f2-56a5-bfb7-528f2e2d7e75" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399774 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-14,$ and the leading coefficient is $7.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-14}{\\text{factor of }7}.\n$$\n\nOf the given answer choices, only $-2=\\frac{-2}{1}$ takes the above form since the numerator $(-2)$ is a factor of $-14$ and the denominator $(1)$ is a factor of $7.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399775 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399776 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399775 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399775 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399775 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399775 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186399780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399775 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399775 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399775 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186399780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399775 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "06a9f0fc-0a9d-5636-9f7e-da5babeb1d8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186399776 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{7}{2}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759