/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433667 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433667 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186433634 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433667 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "60877b9d-92c5-51c5-86d7-d416451d8505" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433636 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433669 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186433662 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433669 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433669 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433669 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186433649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433669 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "82b73eeb-28f6-544a-b4a5-e943633f0b85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433672 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186433667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433672 #edb/ref 1069 "q-14143" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433672 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433672 #edb/ref 1071 "For the polynomial $f(x)=4{x}^{7}-18{x}^{2}+26x-12,$ how many *potential* roots are given by the rational roots theorem?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433672 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2e2ad5ba-576c-5412-b8cf-f6ef173b3a57" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186433672 #edb/ref 1074 "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $-12,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6$ and $\\pm 12.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $4,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2$ and $\\pm 4.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm \\frac{1}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{2}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{2}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{3}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{4}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{4}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{6}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{6}{4}, \\\\ \\pm \\frac{12}{1}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{12}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we reduce the fractions to lowest terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{1}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, & \\pm \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, & \\pm \\frac{3}{4}, \\\\ \\pm 4, & \\pm 2, & \\pm 1, \\\\ \\pm 6, & \\pm 3, & \\pm \\frac{3}{2}, \\\\ \\pm 12, & \\pm 6, & \\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that some potential roots are duplicates (shown above in red). Removing these duplicates, and listing those remaining in order from least to greatest in absolute value, we have\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{4},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{4},\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4,\\,\\pm 6,\\,\\pm 12.\n$$\n\nThis makes a total of $20$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 10=20.$)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436910 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436910 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436910 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186436919 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436910 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436910 #edb/ref 1045 "The Rational Roots Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436910 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186436918 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436910 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186436910 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050429 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759